On Mon, Nov 16, 2015 at 10:07 PM, Carter T. Butts <buttsc@uci.edu> wrote:

> Hi, Michael -
>
> There are many ways to make such comparisons, although the most direct is
> to examine the quantiles of the observed graph statistics in the
> distribution of simulated statistics under your proposed model.  Testing a
> joint hypothesis is more complex, and will depend on how the hypothesis is
> defined; a good example is the classic omnibus triad census test, of which
> you can find a discussion in Wasserman and Faust (1994).  (One need not use
> Gaussian approximations in a modern setting: one can just simulate the
> distribution of the test statistic under one's proposed model.)  If you
> want a more elaborate approach, you could also consider the quantile
> modeling methods in Butts (Sociological Methodology, 2011); see the
> comments in section 5.1 about using a general probability model (rather
> than a CUG model) to produce reference quantiles.  I would not recommend
> this for basic hypothesis testing, but the approach can be useful in
> meta-analytic settings where one has multiple networks from a possibly
> heterogeneous population.
>
> Hope that helps,
>
> -Carter
